For a set of embeddings $\{\mathbf{h}_i\}_{i=1}^{n}$, we first normalize each embedding along its feature dimension,
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{\mathbf{h}}_i = \frac{\mathbf{h}_i}{\max(\lVert\mathbf{h}_i\rVert_2,\varepsilon)},
\end{equation}
where $\varepsilon>0$ is a numerical floor that prevents division by zero. We then compute a linear score from each normalized embedding and apply softmax across the set:
\begin{equation}
s_i = \mathbf{w}^{\top}\widetilde{\mathbf{h}}_i,
\qquad
\alpha_i = \frac{\exp(s_i/\tau)}{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\exp(s_j/\tau)}.
\end{equation}
The temperature $\tau>0$ divides the scores before softmax; increasing it makes the weights more uniform, whereas decreasing it concentrates weight on the highest-scoring embeddings. The pooled representation is
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{z} = \sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_i\widetilde{\mathbf{h}}_i.
\end{equation}
Thus, embedding normalization precedes both scoring and aggregation, and the pooled vector is not normalized again.

